86573210 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186572975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573210 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573210 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573210 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573210 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573210 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186572975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573210 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a9c57c23-5852-5310-9404-decf58de586e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573256 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573256 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ac40a4c-aba2-5c77-8ace-67895a16cfed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573256 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that as $n\\to 0,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{1/n}=e\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n} & ={lim}_{n\\to 0}{[{(1+n)}^{1/n}]}^{4/7} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{1/n}]}^{4/7} \\\\  & ={e}^{4/7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573260 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $a$ given that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\n$$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c781737-7cc2-58de-b406-ca857cb292ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573260 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we let \n\n$$\nL=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=L.\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(a).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand so,  $L=-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $L=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =4 \\\\ a & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573289 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573289 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573289 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573289 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{2}}g(x)}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573289 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8db2f83d-f6c6-583a-9190-f65543a5bd2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573289 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{({x}^{2}-2x-8)}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x-8)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}(2x-\\frac{4x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{2}}(\\frac{2x({x}^{2}-2x-8)-(4x-4)}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}(2x({x}^{2}-2x-8)-(4x-4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}(2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-16x-4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x+2)}{({x}^{2}-2x-8)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x+2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573290 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f2aebee-176b-5447-94e6-cdb44a5559c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573290 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,(x){|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}x(\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({e}^{2})^{3})\\,({e}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})\\,(1)]-3{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6})-3x{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}-3({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573327 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573327 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02ef4fc2-6aff-5366-8724-8878c0dcf4a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573327 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186250284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573331 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573331 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "142833f0-e6bd-5cd5-a5c3-41884f888f14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573331 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573332 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "17953158-dc5b-538f-b42d-38a61d6989eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186250285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573335 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573335 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f7491a9-cbc7-503f-a32b-7dc183ace05c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573335 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186573260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "26d7382c-5c4